{"id":"cf7da027-3b9c-475a-8713-592133c55a2c","kind":"note","ref":{"span":"61e0abbae122bb08","key":"bablr1","file":"packages/editor/shell/preview-profile.ts","baseline":{"blob":"fc5a418296c7020db864cc065f31a1f94f5d8683","start":0,"end":100},"shape":{"type":"ImportDeclaration","atoms":["import","type","{","CpuProfile",",","ProfileEntry",",","ProfilerTrace","}","from","\"","@brianjenkins94/editor-api/cpuprofile","\""]},"context":{"after":"a4eb2d27eddc588a"}},"payload":{"text":"CPU profiles of a preview window's page, asked for: its title bar's Profile button, or `preview.profile` over the hub,\nwhich agents use. Each is published on `preview.profiled`, for the workbench to keep where each of the app's functions'\ntime went with the run (workbench/profile-times.ts), which the insights extension shows on the code.\n\nIt's the JS Self-Profiling API: a `Profiler` made in the preview page's own realm samples that page's thread. A\nProfiler can be made only in a document served with `Document-Policy: js-profiling`, which the service worker adds to\nevery preview response (coi-serviceworker.js). A preview shares the shell's thread (same origin), so the editor's own\nfunctions can show up in its samples; the app's are the ones whose url is under the preview's `/__virtual__/` path.","wrote":["Brian Jenkins"]},"author":"local","createdAt":"2026-11-22T17:45:03.554Z","updatedAt":"2026-11-22T17:45:03.554Z"}
